WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.640
Robert Downey Jr.'s career may have reached&nbsp;
astronomical heights when he was cast as Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.720
Stark, a.k.a. Iron Man, in the very first&nbsp;
film of the Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.400
but he already had a long career before&nbsp;
that, with a ton of ups and downs.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.600
So you might be surprised to find out&nbsp;
that he doesn't consider his best film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.880
to be "Iron Man," or any of his&nbsp;
Marvel blockbuster appearances!

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.360
Downey started out doing roles in '80s comedies&nbsp;
like "Weird Science," and then proved his dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.240
acting chops in "Less than Zero." In the '90s,&nbsp;
Downey's film credits were a bit hit or miss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.080
On the one hand, he received an Oscar nomination&nbsp;
for his portrayal of Charlie Chaplin in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.040
biopic "Chaplin," and his starring role in the&nbsp;
1995 historical drama "Restoration" was well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.600
received by critics, plus he had a recurring&nbsp;
role on the hit TV series "Ally McBeal."

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.120
On the other hand, movies like the&nbsp;
sappy rom-com "Heart and Souls" and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.200
underwhelming thriller "In Dreams" weren't&nbsp;
a big help in moving his career forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.440
And after a serious drug addiction derailed&nbsp;
his career in the late '90s and early 2000's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.160
Downey Jr. struggled to find work, as few people&nbsp;
in Hollywood were willing to take a risk on him.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.520
That may be one reason why, during&nbsp;
a 2020 appearance on the "Joe Rogan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.560
Experience" podcast, Downey Jr.&nbsp;
said that his best film is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.240
the one that had the most importance&nbsp;
to his career: "Kiss Kiss Bang Bang."

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.160
Released in 2005, "Kiss Kiss Bang&nbsp;
Bang" is an action-packed dark comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.840
produced by Downey's friend Joel Silver.&nbsp;
Downey told Rogan that it was, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.840
"in some ways the best film I've ever done."

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.840
"It came out, and it bombed, but Jon Favreau&nbsp;
saw it, and he said, 'This guy could do an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.920
action movie.' And that wound up being my&nbsp;
calling card into the Marvel Universe."

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.680
Jon Favreau, of course, directed and&nbsp;
co-starred in "Iron Man" in 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.160
and he gave Downey Jr. his next big launch into&nbsp;
stardom. And though "Kiss Kiss Bang Bang" only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.600
made $15.7 million worldwide at the box office,&nbsp;
it was a big hit with critics, with a Certified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.000
Fresh score of 86% on Rotten Tomatoes to&nbsp;
go along with an audience score of 87%.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.480
"Kiss Kiss Bang Bang" was also written and&nbsp;
directed by Shane Black, who Downey later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.640
brought in to write and direct "Iron Man 3"&nbsp;
after Favreau bowed out of the franchise.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.400
Like Downey, Black was also in a career&nbsp;
slump before "Kiss Kiss Bang Bang."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.080
The writer/director had previously found&nbsp;
great success writing and producing movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.520
like "Lethal Weapon" and "The Last Boy&nbsp;
Scout," but after "The Long Kiss Goodnight"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.600
bombed at the box office in 1996, he&nbsp;
struggled to find work in Hollywood.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.280
"Kiss Kiss Bang Bang" was Black's attempt&nbsp;
to start over by branching out from straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.880
action movies. The fact that he himself&nbsp;
had trouble finding people to take a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.480
on him may have influenced his decision&nbsp;
to give Downey a second chance as well.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.720
Thankfully for all of us, Black took the risk and&nbsp;
hired Downey to play one of the main characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.280
Though "Kiss Kiss Bang Bang" didn't do very well&nbsp;
at the box office, it did become a cult classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.200
The quality of the film helped revive both&nbsp;
Black and Downey's careers, and set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.840
wheels in motion for Marvel to also take a risk&nbsp;
on casting Downey in the first "Iron Man" film.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.840
It's hard to believe now, after Downey Jr.'s&nbsp;
breakout performance as Tony Stark set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.600
stage for the Marvel Cinematic Universe to&nbsp;
become the biggest film franchise in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.200
but when he was first cast in "Iron&nbsp;
Man" in 2006, there were many who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.280
skeptical Downey Jr. could be trusted with&nbsp;
the responsibility. His work on "Kiss Kiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.760
Bang Bang" was good enough to convince Marvel to&nbsp;
take the chance, though, and the rest is history.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.920
No wonder Downey thinks it's his best film!

